Skip the static-libstdc++ link probe for the Kandelo wasm32 target.

Kandelo configures SpiderMonkey as wasm32-unknown-linux-musl, so Mozilla
runs its Linux-only probe: link a small C++ program and look for a NEEDED
libstdc++/libc++ entry with llvm-objdump. A static wasm executable has no
dynamic dependencies, so the probe reports static libstdc++ linkage and
configure dies. Kandelo links the sysroot's static libc++ by design, so the
probe does not apply to wasm32.

--- a/build/moz.configure/flags.configure
+++ b/build/moz.configure/flags.configure
@@ -21,6 +21,7 @@
     extra_toolchain_flags,
     linker_ldflags,
     llvm_objdump,
+    target,
     when=target_has_linux_kernel,
 )
 @checking("whether we're trying to statically link with libstdc++")
@@ -32,8 +33,12 @@
     extra_toolchain_flags,
     linker_ldflags,
     llvm_objdump,
+    target,
 ):
     with create_temporary_file(suffix=".out") as bin_path:
+        if target.cpu == "wasm32":
+            return False
+
         # This test is quite conservative: it assumes dynamic linkage if the
         # compilation step fails or if the binary format is not supported. But
         # it still detects basic issues.
